Why This Is Asked
- Tests dynamic graph state and a non-trivial at-query metric.

Key Concepts
- Dynamic adjacency via hash sets, on-demand intersection, edge-id tracking, amortized performance.

Code Example
```javascript
class DynamicGraph {
  constructor(){ this.adj = new Map(); this.edges = new Map(); }
  addEdge(u,v,id){ if(!this.adj.has(u)) this.adj.set(u,new Set()); if(!this.adj.has(v)) this.adj.set(v,new Set()); this.adj.get(u).add(v); this.adj.get(v).add(u); this.edges.set(id,[u,v]); }
  removeEdge(id){ const e = this.edges.get(id); if(!e) return; const [u,v] = e; this.adj.get(u).delete(v); this.adj.get(v).delete(u); this.edges.delete(id); }
  twoStepPaths(u,v){ if(u===v) return 0; const A = this.adj.get(u) || new Set(); const B = this.adj.get(v) || new Set(); if(A.size < B.size){ let c=0; for(const x of A) if(B.has(x)) c++; return c; } else { let c=0; for(const x of B) if(A.has(x)) c++; return c; } }
}
```

Follow-up Questions
- How would you extend to directed graphs or multi-edges? Impact on correctness and complexity?
- What if you need to maintain this metric for many (u, v) pairs offline at once—any batching strategies?

